    "long_instructions": "# Investigate\r\n\r\nRun the code again and look at where the Painter stops.\r\n\r\nTrace it: Before Check 3 runs, how much paint does the Painter have? Finish the comment on Check 3 with your answer.\r\n\r\nTinker: In every check, move the `if can_move():` line and the `move()` under it one level to the left, so they line up with `if has_paint():`. Run the code again. Where does the Painter stop now? What changed?\r\n\r\nTinker: Put the indents back. Then delete one `take_paint()` line from the setup and run the code again. Where does the Painter stop now? Why?\r\n\r\nComment it: Finish the comment on Check 1. Which decision depends on the other one, and how can you tell from the indentation?",
    "start_sources": {
      "files": {
        "0": {
          "id": "0",
          "name": "main.py",
          "contents": "from neighborhood import *\n\n# Painter setup: these lines turn the Painter south and restock it at the bucket.\nturn_left()\nturn_left()\nturn_left()\nmove()\ntake_paint()\ntake_paint()\n\n# Check 1 (finish this comment): \nif has_paint():\n    paint(\"white\")\n    if can_move():\n        move()\n\n# Check 2\nif has_paint():\n    paint(\"white\")\n    if can_move():\n        move()\n\n# Check 3 (finish this comment): \nif has_paint():\n    paint(\"white\")\n    if can_move():\n        move()\n\n# Check 4\nif has_paint():\n    paint(\"white\")\n    if can_move():\n        move()",
          "folderId": "0",
          "active": false
        }
