Y\n3TAHBa3r8d0NYZCILs+kX1mid6pSRBLzUfpalM3ZyS0grWOf2814vEV3rFh8\nuiEwb2WtwXAs4BCVRrHNWiujDOZ64bEBNtKS7LFae+rtlPY7uyzzIPL1xg0z\nfQdiq436m5YXh9K9op6BZeo1qneD9T97P9XyepLJS5ty58Nb475KsoZp1jZK\ntubTx57MHHRzxIZ1IUSJzgUc7Ldl+QZf/Q6PzfKijCfa4gf4pTAu4aLr2igX\n25QcXEhrHfnujRsJ1iS5T3VC1kIq7hJFTMuvS6nyv56ylOXFy4w4O9BFhiU0\n7lXIAlY8+a10liJmZzs2nAFAd8eF+ubl3xhzZr3U6lSfKlS3OZLJgQivudlB\nudQJnm2NF35HvBoW4LYD3KdHQGy94ICXdyKDNdE9/oIvqbJiM4iJL3Ft/gZT\nDSeere1B8EccDrhsgrV9Gxtth+N7k/LuVKQJBg6KfKrxRduDiMkUSizSB/dh\nyVMdC6Zj6GyGlNxgKLGpDI0yin+2MdAdeq5f1ha7NoIiFqVTn+xlsAEJB3b7\nTJYGwRf0SVrj1HZhnREb55DjsUDUaf8UbZuOuwU932lHQY//ZOjjvGOnNl03\nm0HvjC2El6c1l90iGYup4kjYQXvA0AftLRvh679gqQ0WxKJKlP93PoAIPZJ3\nj9jZM1KeBaHpyGDup6qxZVhmRR7ThYgjwhimbtxe3SMSR0WtWLeeyJ857Jxj\nFym5p3r6JXN5uyq71WMUWUfe++ELtj4c44ioNPI9EumNBvNWR023Uf5EHyAz\nrxlPHP3jFzs5l1faC29uxMlIQaaQrG8=\n",
    "teacher_markdown": "Suggested experiments and what they reveal:\r\n\r\n- Unindent can_move() in every check: the program now has two separate decisions. The Painter still paints the same two squares, but it keeps walking after it runs out of paint and stops one square before the cone, two squares farther than before. Separate decisions are both always checked.\r\n- Delete one take_paint(): the Painter has one unit of paint. It paints one square and moves one square, then stops, because the outer check is False. In the separate version, it would still keep walking.\r\n- Both versions paint the same squares. What differs is whether moving depends on having paint.\r\n\r\nMisconceptions to watch for:\r\n\r\n- Thinking the Painter moves in every check. The nested move only happens when the outer check is True.\r\n- Thinking indentation is decoration. One level of indentation turns a separate decision into a dependent one.\r\n\r\nTarget answers for the comments:\r\n\r\n- Check 3: \"The Painter has no paint left, so the outer check is False and nothing inside it runs.\"\r\n- Check 1: \"The move depends on having paint. The if can_move(): line is indented inside the if has_paint(): block, so it is only asked when the Painter has paint.\"\r\n\r\nThis level has no validation. Ask students to show you where the Painter stops for each version."
  },
